2207.3s 177 The clinical presentation of diabetes includes three main categories: Diabetes Insipidus, Type I Diabetes Mellitus, and Type II Diabetes Mellitus.
2207.3s 178 Diabetes Insipidus presents with excessive urination and extreme thirst. Younger patients often experience nausea or vomiting due to frequent urination. Older adults who suffer from diabetes insipidus usually do not show any other symptoms besides increased urination and thirst.
2207.3s 179 Type I Diabetes Mellitus has classic symptoms such as unexplained weight loss, fatigue, blurred vision, dry mouth, frequent urination, and extreme thirst. Patients also tend to be more irritable than usual.
2207.3s 180 Type II Diabetes Mellitus typically causes less noticeable symptoms like feeling tired all the time, having trouble controlling their blood sugar levels, being thirsty frequently, needing to go to the bathroom many times during the night, and experiencing blurry vision.
